  "worked_solution": "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x+y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x)+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=7,$ $x=1,$ and $y=3,$ and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x)+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ 7\\cdot (2(1)\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1))+(1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1}\\cdot 7+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ 0+7+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ 7+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
